This project depends on, but does not vendor, the following. Their own licences
apply and are not superseded by this file:

  Depth Anything V2 checkpoints  - see the model card on Hugging Face; the
                                   Small/Base checkpoints and the Large
                                   checkpoint are released under different
                                   terms, and the Large checkpoint's terms
                                   should be checked before any commercial use.
  PyTorch, transformers          - BSD-3-Clause / Apache-2.0
  NumPy, SciPy, Pillow, rasterio - BSD-3-Clause and similar
  matplotlib                     - PSF-based licence
  Copernicus DEM GLO-30          - ESA/Airbus terms; free for most uses with
                                   attribution, check before redistribution
  Mapbox Terrain-RGB             - an encoding convention, used here by
                                   specification only; no Mapbox code is used
  scikit-image                   - BSD-3-Clause. Marching squares and
                                   Douglas-Peucker in mesh/regularize.py
  CNES Bulldozer                 - Apache-2.0. Called as a library by
                                   dsm/dtm.py; no Bulldozer code is copied
  trimesh, pymeshlab             - MIT / GPL-3.0 respectively. pymeshlab is
                                   OPTIONAL and its GPL terms are the reason:
                                   mesh/decimate.py degrades to a strided
                                   rebuild without it, so no configuration of
                                   this project requires linking GPL code
  OpenStreetMap data             - Open Database Licence (ODbL) 1.0. Data only,
                                   fetched at run time and cached; derived
                                   works carry share-alike obligations, so a
                                   run made with --osm must attribute OSM
  Overpass API                   - queried under its usage policy; data is ODbL
  Microsoft TRELLIS.2            - MIT. Invoked as a subprocess/library by
                                   mesh/trellis.py. No TRELLIS code is copied;
                                   see ORIGINALITY below for the single
                                   interoperability exception
  SAM 2 checkpoints              - see the model card; used for structural
                                   instance segmentation

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORIGINALITY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every line under traksha/, tests/, scripts/ and web/src/ was written for this
project. No source file is a copy, a fork, a port or a lightly edited
derivative of another codebase, and nothing third-party is vendored into this
tree. The dependencies above are *called*, at their public interfaces, exactly
as any application calls a library.

That claim is checkable rather than asserted: no file in this repository
carries another party's copyright header or SPDX identifier, and no directory
under traksha/ contains third-party sources.

Two clarifications, because they are the places a reader would reasonably ask.

  1. INTEROPERABILITY, NOT COPYING.  mesh/trellis.py contains `frame()`, which
     reproduces the *behaviour* of TRELLIS.2's input normalisation - centre,
     isotropic scale into [-0.5, 0.5], Z-up to Y-up axis swap. It is written
     independently, in this project's own idiom, with its own numerical guard,
     and it exists for one reason: the transform must be INVERTED on this side
     so the geometry that comes back can be compared against the geometry that
     was sent. Without the inverse there is no way to assert that a refiner
     preserved a measurement, and that assertion is the whole safety argument
     of the stage. This is the same posture as the Mapbox Terrain-RGB entry
     above - a documented convention implemented to specification, not code
     taken from anyone.

  2. ALGORITHMS ARE NOT AUTHORSHIP.  Marching squares, Douglas-Peucker, quadric
     edge collapse, guided filtering, drape-cloth filtering and IRLS are
     published algorithms and are used as such. Where a well-tested
     implementation exists it is called (scikit-image, SciPy, Bulldozer,
     PyMeshLab); where one is written out here - the scanline polygon fill in
     data/osm.py, the rectilinear regularisation in mesh/regularize.py, the
     Douglas-Peucker fallback - it is this project's own implementation, and
     the docstring says why it was written rather than imported.

Research prior art is cited in README section 10 and throughout section 6.
Citing a paper is not using its code: of the systems evaluated in section 6.2,
threefiner was integrated and then removed (section 6.2e), Sat3DGen was
integrated and then removed (section 6.2f), and Sat2City v1 and v2 have never
published any code at all (section 6.2g). What section 6.2g runs is TRELLIS.2,
under its own MIT licence, through its own public pipeline class.
